isFibration(ToricMap) -- whether a toric map is a fibration
***********************************************************

  * Function: *note isFibration: isFibration_lpToricMap_rp,
  * Usage: 
        isFibration f
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note toric map: ToricMap,, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, that is *note true:
        (Macaulay2Doc)true, if the map is a fibration

Description
===========

A proper morphism $f : X \to Y$ is a fibration if $f_*(OO_X) = OO_Y$.  A proper
toric map is a fibration if and only if the underlying map of lattices is a
surjection. For more information, see Proposition 2.1 in
deCataldo-Migliorini-Mustata, "The combinatorics and topology of proper toric
maps" arXiv:1407.3497 (see https://arxiv.org/abs/1407.3497 ).

We illustrate this method on the projection from the first *note Hirzebruch
surface: hirzebruchSurface_lpZZ_rp, to the *note projective line:
toricProjectiveSpace_lpZZ_rp,.

+-----------------------------------------------+
|i1 : X = hirzebruchSurface 1;                  |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i2 : Y = toricProjectiveSpace 1;               |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i3 : f = map(Y, X, matrix{{1 ,0}})             |
|                                               |
|o3 = | 1 0 |                                   |
|                                               |
|o3 : ToricMap Y <--- X                         |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i4 : isFibration f                             |
|                                               |
|o4 = true                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i5 : assert (isWellDefined f and isFibration f)|
+-----------------------------------------------+

Here is an example of a proper map that is not a fibration.

+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : Z = weightedProjectiveSpace {1, 1, 2};                       |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : g = map(Z, X, matrix{{1, 0}, {0, -2}})                       |
|                                                                  |
|o7 = | 1 0  |                                                     |
|     | 0 -2 |                                                     |
|                                                                  |
|o7 : ToricMap Z <--- X                                            |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : isFibration g                                                |
|                                                                  |
|o8 = false                                                        |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : assert (isWellDefined g and isProper g and not isFibration g)|
+------------------------------------------------------------------+

To avoid repeating a computation, the package caches the result in the *note
CacheTable: (Macaulay2Doc)CacheTable, of the toric map.

See also
========

  * *note working with toric maps: working with toric maps, -- information
    about toric maps and the induced operations
  * *note isProper(ToricMap): isProper_lpToricMap_rp, -- whether a toric map is
    proper

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note isFibration(ToricMap): isFibration_lpToricMap_rp, -- whether a toric
    map is a fibration
