isProper(ToricMap) -- whether a toric map is proper
***************************************************

  * Function: *note isProper: isProper_lpToricMap_rp,
  * Usage: 
        isProper f
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note toric map: ToricMap,, 
  * Outputs:
      * a *note Boolean value: (Macaulay2Doc)Boolean,, that is *note true:
        (Macaulay2Doc)true, if the map is proper

Description
===========

A morphism of varieties is proper if it is universally closed. For a toric map
$f : X \to Y$ corresponding to the map $g : N_X \to N_Y$ of lattices, this is
equivalent to the preimage of the support of the target fan under $g$ being
equal to the support of the source fan. For more information about this
equivalence, see Theorem 3.4.11 in Cox-Little-Schenck's Toric Varieties.

We illustrate this method on the projection from the second *note Hirzebruch
surface: hirzebruchSurface_lpZZ_rp, to the *note projective line:
toricProjectiveSpace_lpZZ_rp,.

+---------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : X = hirzebruchSurface 2;                      |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : Y = toricProjectiveSpace 1;                   |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : f = map(Y, X, matrix {{1,0}})                 |
|                                                   |
|o3 = | 1 0 |                                       |
|                                                   |
|o3 : ToricMap Y <--- X                             |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : isProper f                                    |
|                                                   |
|o4 = true                                          |
+---------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : assert (isWellDefined f and source f === X and|
|         target f === Y and isProper f)            |
+---------------------------------------------------+

The second example shows that the *note projection: NormalToricVariety ^ Array,
from the *note blow-up: toricBlowup_lpList_cmNormalToricVariety_cmList_rp, of
the origin in the affine plane to *note affine plane: affineSpace_lpZZ_rp, is
proper.

+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : A = affineSpace 2;                                           |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : B = toricBlowup({0,1}, A);                                   |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : g = B^[]                                                     |
|                                                                  |
|o8 = | 1 0 |                                                      |
|     | 0 1 |                                                      |
|                                                                  |
|o8 : ToricMap A <--- B                                            |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : isProper g                                                   |
|                                                                  |
|o9 = true                                                         |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : assert(isWellDefined g and g == map(A, B, 1) and isProper g)|
+------------------------------------------------------------------+

The natural inclusion of the *note affine plane: affineSpace_lpZZ_rp, into the
*note projective plane: toricProjectiveSpace_lpZZ_rp, is not proper.

+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i11 : A = affineSpace 2;                                          |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i12 : P = toricProjectiveSpace 2;                                 |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i13 : f = map(P, A, 1)                                            |
|                                                                  |
|o13 = | 1 0 |                                                     |
|      | 0 1 |                                                     |
|                                                                  |
|o13 : ToricMap P <--- A                                           |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i14 : isProper f                                                  |
|                                                                  |
|o14 = false                                                       |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i15 : isDominant f                                                |
|                                                                  |
|o15 = true                                                        |
+------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i16 : assert (isWellDefined f and not isProper f and isDominant f)|
+------------------------------------------------------------------+

To avoid repeating a computation, the package caches the result in the *note
CacheTable: (Macaulay2Doc)CacheTable, of the toric map.

See also
========

  * *note working with toric maps: working with toric maps, -- information
    about toric maps and the induced operations
  * *note map(NormalToricVariety,NormalToricVariety,Matrix):
    map_lpNormalToricVariety_cmNormalToricVariety_cmMatrix_rp, -- make a
    torus-equivariant map between normal toric varieties
  * *note map(NormalToricVariety,NormalToricVariety,ZZ):
    map_lpNormalToricVariety_cmNormalToricVariety_cmZZ_rp, -- make a
    torus-equivariant map between normal toric varieties
  * *note isComplete(NormalToricVariety): isComplete_lpNormalToricVariety_rp,
    -- whether a toric variety is complete

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note isProper(ToricMap): isProper_lpToricMap_rp, -- whether a toric map is
    proper
