matrix(ToricMap) -- get the underlying map of lattices
******************************************************

  * Function: *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)matrix,
  * Usage: 
        g = matrix f
  * Inputs:
      * f, a *note toric map: ToricMap,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note Degree: (Macaulay2Doc)matrix_lpList_rp, => ..., default value
        null, unused
  * Outputs:
      * g, a *note matrix: (Macaulay2Doc)Matrix,, over the *note integers:
        (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,

Description
===========

Every toric map $f : X \to Y$ corresponds to a unique map $g : N_X \to N_Y$ of
lattices such that, for every cone $\sigma$ in the fan of $X$, there is a cone
in the fan of $Y$ that contains the image $g(\sigma)$.  For more information on
this correspondence, see Theorem 3.3.4 in Cox-Little-Schenck's Toric Varieties.
This method returns an integer matrix representing $g$.

We illustrate how to access this defining feature of a toric map with the
projection from the second *note Hirzebruch surface: hirzebruchSurface_lpZZ_rp,
to the *note projective line: toricProjectiveSpace_lpZZ_rp,.

+-----------------------------------------------+
|i1 : X = hirzebruchSurface 2;                  |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i2 : Y = toricProjectiveSpace 1;               |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i3 : f = map(Y, X, matrix {{1, 0}})            |
|                                               |
|o3 = | 1 0 |                                   |
|                                               |
|o3 : ToricMap Y <--- X                         |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i4 : g = matrix f                              |
|                                               |
|o4 = | 1 0 |                                   |
|                                               |
|              1       2                        |
|o4 : Matrix ZZ  <-- ZZ                         |
+-----------------------------------------------+
|i5 : assert (isWellDefined f and ring g === ZZ)|
+-----------------------------------------------+

An *note inclusion map: NormalToricVariety _us Array, into the Cartesian square
of a normal toric variety corresponds to matrix having identity and zero
blocks.

+---------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : X2 = X ** X;                                        |
+---------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : iota0 = X2_[0]                                      |
|                                                         |
|o7 = | 1 0 |                                             |
|     | 0 1 |                                             |
|     | 0 0 |                                             |
|     | 0 0 |                                             |
|                                                         |
|o7 : ToricMap X2 <--- X                                  |
+---------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : assert (isWellDefined iota0 and source iota0 === X) |
+---------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : iota1 = X2_[1]                                      |
|                                                         |
|o9 = | 0 0 |                                             |
|     | 0 0 |                                             |
|     | 1 0 |                                             |
|     | 0 1 |                                             |
|                                                         |
|o9 : ToricMap X2 <--- X                                  |
+---------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : assert (isWellDefined iota1 and source iota1 === X)|
+---------------------------------------------------------+

In a well-defined toric map, the number of rows in the underlying matrix must
equal the dimension of the target and the number of columns must equal the
dimension of the source.

+------------------------------------+
|i11 : assert (numColumns g == dim X)|
+------------------------------------+
|i12 : assert (numRows g == dim Y)   |
+------------------------------------+

The output display for toric maps is inherited the underlying map of lattices.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i13 : code (net, ToricMap)                                                   |
|                                                                             |
|o13 = -- code for method: net(ToricMap)                                      |
|      ../../../../../Macaulay2/packages/NormalToricVarieties/ToricMaps.m2:10:|
|      16-10:33: --source code:                                               |
|      net ToricMap := f -> net matrix f                                      |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Since this is a defining attribute of a toric map, no computation is required.

See also
========

  * *note working with toric maps: working with toric maps, -- information
    about toric maps and the induced operations
  * *note source(ToricMap): source_lpToricMap_rp, -- get the source of the map
  * *note target(ToricMap): target_lpToricMap_rp, -- get the target of the map
  * *note isWellDefined(ToricMap): isWellDefined_lpToricMap_rp, -- whether a
    toric map is well defined
  * *note map(NormalToricVariety,NormalToricVariety,Matrix):
    map_lpNormalToricVariety_cmNormalToricVariety_cmMatrix_rp, -- make a
    torus-equivariant map between normal toric varieties
  * *note NormalToricVariety ** NormalToricVariety: NormalToricVariety _st_st
    NormalToricVariety, -- make the Cartesian product of two normal toric
    varieties
  * *note NormalToricVariety _ Array: NormalToricVariety _us Array, -- make a
    canonical inclusion into a product

Ways to use this method:
========================

  * *note matrix(ToricMap): matrix_lpToricMap_rp, -- get the underlying map of
    lattices
