working with toric maps -- information about toric maps and the induced operations
**********************************************************************************

A toric map is a morphism $f : X \to Y$ between normal toric varieties that
induces a morphism of algebraic groups $g : T_X \to T_Y$ such that $f$ is
$T_X$-equivariant with respect to the $T_X$-action on $Y$ induced by $g$.
Every toric map $f : X \to Y$ corresponds to a unique map $f_N : N_X \to N_Y$
between the underlying lattices.

Although the primary method for creating a toric map is *note
map(NormalToricVariety,NormalToricVariety,Matrix):
map_lpNormalToricVariety_cmNormalToricVariety_cmMatrix_rp,, there are a few
other constructors.

Making toric maps
=================

  * *note map(NormalToricVariety,NormalToricVariety,Matrix):
    map_lpNormalToricVariety_cmNormalToricVariety_cmMatrix_rp, -- make a
    torus-equivariant map between normal toric varieties
  * *note id _ NormalToricVariety: id _us NormalToricVariety, -- make the
    identity map from a NormalToricVariety to itself
  * *note NormalToricVariety ^ Array: NormalToricVariety ^ Array, -- make a
    canonical projection map
  * *note NormalToricVariety _ Array: NormalToricVariety _us Array, -- make a
    canonical inclusion into a product
  * *note diagonalToricMap: diagonalToricMap, -- make a diagonal map into a
    Cartesian product
  * *note ToricMap: ToricMap, -- the class of all torus-equivariant maps
    between normal toric varieties
  * *note isWellDefined(ToricMap): isWellDefined_lpToricMap_rp, -- whether a
    toric map is well defined

Having made a *note toric map: ToricMap,, one can access its basic invariants
or test for some elementary properties by using the following methods.

Determining attributes and properties of toric maps
===================================================

  * *note source(ToricMap): source_lpToricMap_rp, -- get the source of the map
  * *note target(ToricMap): target_lpToricMap_rp, -- get the target of the map
  * *note matrix(ToricMap): matrix_lpToricMap_rp, -- get the underlying map of
    lattices
  * *note ToricMap * ToricMap: ToricMap _st ToricMap, -- make the composition
    of two toric maps
  * *note isProper(ToricMap): isProper_lpToricMap_rp, -- whether a toric map is
    proper
  * *note isFibration(ToricMap): isFibration_lpToricMap_rp, -- whether a toric
    map is a fibration
  * *note isDominant(ToricMap): isDominant_lpToricMap_rp, -- whether a toric
    map is dominant
  * *note isSurjective(ToricMap): isSurjective_lpToricMap_rp, -- whether a
    toric map is surjective

Several functorial aspects of normal toric varieties are also available.

Functorial aspects of toric maps
================================

  * *note weilDivisorGroup(ToricMap): weilDivisorGroup_lpToricMap_rp, -- make
    the induced map between groups of Weil divisors
  * *note classGroup(ToricMap): classGroup_lpToricMap_rp, -- make the induced
    map between class groups
  * *note cartierDivisorGroup(ToricMap): cartierDivisorGroup_lpToricMap_rp, --
    make the induced map between groups of Cartier divisors.
  * *note picardGroup(ToricMap): picardGroup_lpToricMap_rp, -- make the induced
    map between Picard groups
  * *note pullback(ToricMap,ToricDivisor):
    pullback_lpToricMap_cmToricDivisor_rp, -- make the pullback of a Cartier
    divisor under a toric map
  * *note pullback(ToricMap,CoherentSheaf):
    pullback_lpToricMap_cmCoherentSheaf_rp, -- make the pullback of a coherent
    sheaf under a toric map
  * *note inducedMap(ToricMap): inducedMap_lpToricMap_rp, -- make the induced
    map between total coordinate rings (a.k.a. Cox rings)
  * *note ideal(ToricMap): ideal_lpToricMap_rp, -- make the ideal defining the
    closure of the image

